Ours.  MIT-SHM is not built (HasShm NO).

ProcShmAttach attaches whatever segment id a client names, with the
server's rights -- root, under xdm and xinit -- and R6.3 cannot learn a
client's uid to check the segment's permissions (a TCP client has none).
Any client, remote ones included, could have the server read a segment
into a drawable (ShmPutImage, then GetImage) or write into one
(ShmGetImage).  Clients that use MIT-SHM test for it (MITSHM) and fall
back to ordinary image requests; libXext loses XShm.o.

--- a/config/cf/amix.cf
+++ b/config/cf/amix.cf
@@ -56,8 +56,10 @@
 #define BuildLBX		NO
 #define XprtServer		NO
 #define XnestServer		NO
-/* MIT-SHM: safe with the 68040/68060 kernel's shared-memory fixes (README) */
-#define HasShm			YES
+/* MIT-SHM off: ShmAttach attaches any segment id a client names, with the
+ * server's rights (root), and R6.3 cannot learn a client's uid to check the
+ * segment's permissions (README) */
+#define HasShm			NO
 /* colours from rgb.txt: the server's rgb reader is Amix's classic dbm, whose
  * .dir/.pag format no build-host tool writes */
 #define UseRgbTxt		YES
